ve T4 or T6 or T7. So maybe not useful. So discarding T5 is also safe.\n\nBut which is better? Discarding W9 or T5? Let's think about the frequency of those tiles. W9 has 2 left, T5 has 4 left. So if I discard W9, there are two more, so others might need it. T5 has four left, so maybe less chance of being needed.\n\nAlternatively, maybe keeping W9 for a possible meld. But since I don't have W8 or W7, it's not part of any chow. So discarding W9 is safe.\n\nSo the best action might be to discard W9. That's one of the valid options. Let me check the valid actions again: yes, Play W9 is listed.\n\nAnother thought: my hand has two F4s. If I can keep them as a pair, maybe that's useful. Also, if I can form a concealed hand, which is worth 4 points. To do that, I need to not take any exposed tiles. Since I'm discarding, and my melds are empty, as long as I don't take any exposed melds, I can keep it concealed. So discarding W9 would help in that.\n\nIn conclusion, discarding W9 seems like the best option here. It's a single tile not part of any potential melds, and discarding it doesn't hinder my ability to form melds later.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The best move is to discard W9 as it is a single tile not part of any potential melds and minimizes risk.\nReason: Discarding W9 removes a non-vital tile, preserving potential for forming chows (T1-T2-T3, B4-B5-B6) and pairs (F4). It maintains a concealed hand for scoring advantages while avoiding aiding opponents with critical tiles.\nAnswer: Play W9"}
