ext step is to form other melds.\n\nIf I keep T8 and W3, maybe I can form Pungs. But if I discard one of them, I reduce the chance. For example, if I discard T8, I have one left, which can be part of a pair or a Pung. But if I keep both, I need one more.\n\nSimilarly for W3. So maybe the best is to discard a tile that's not needed for any meld. F2, T5, B1 are candidates.\n\nLooking at the unshown tiles, F2 has 1 left. So if I discard F2, there's a chance someone else might need it, but since I don't have any F2 in my hand except one, discarding it is safe.\n\nT5 is a single tile. Not part of any meld. Discarding it is safe.\n\nB1 is a single tile. Same.\n\nSo, between these, which is the best to discard? Maybe F2, since it's a Wind tile, and the Seat Wind is South. If I have a Pung of South Wind (F2), that would be a scoring pattern (Seat Wind). But I have only one F2. If I discard it, I can't form that Pung. But since I only have one, it's not helpful anyway. So discarding F2 is safe.\n\nAlternatively, keeping F2 might help if I can form a Pung later. But with only one, it's not possible. So discarding F2 is safe.\n\nSo, the best options are to discard F2, T5, or B1. Among these, which is the least useful. Since F2 is a Wind tile, which might be more valuable if kept for possible Pung, but with only one, it's not. So maybe discard F2.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the T5 or B1. Let's see. T5 is a suited tile. If someone else needs it for a Chow, but I don't have any Chows involving T5. So discarding T5 is safe.\n\nBut the key is to maximize the chances of forming the required melds. If I discard F2, I can focus on forming the T1-T2-T3 Chi and then work on Pungs. If I keep F2, it's just a single tile.\n\nSo, the best move is to discard F2. That would be the least impactful tile, allowing me to form the necessary melds with the remaining tiles.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [Discarding F2 is optimal as it is a single tile with no immediate use, while retaining T1-T2-T3 for a 